  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how material can move from the bloodstream into brain tissue at the level of a capillary and how this may influence neural function. On the left, an artery branches into narrow capillaries wrapped by blue perivascular cells, and a zoomed cross-section shows the capillary wall in more detail. The vessel contains a red blood cell, while the wall is formed by endothelial cells joined by tight junctions and surrounded by a pericyte layer, emphasizing the blood–brain barrier structure.\n\nFrom this capillary cross-section, three transport routes are highlighted. One route, labeled receptor-mediated transcytosis, is linked to insulin signaling and downstream effects such as neurotransmitter release, mitochondrial function, and synaptic plasticity. A second route, adsorptive-mediated transcytosis, is associated with albumin entry and broader tissue responses including TGFβ signaling, astrocytic transformation, extracellular matrix or perineuronal net modulation, excitatory synaptogenesis, and plasticity. A third pathway, paracellular diffusion, refers to movement between cells and is tied to changes in ion concentrations such as calcium, magnesium, potassium, sodium, and chloride. Overall, the figure connects barrier transport mechanisms with altered neuronal excitability and synaptic function.",
